{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7L8co2zimciYjYVwuhz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before I draft, a few questions. Your answers will change what the email says:\n\n1. **The 11 remote hires who live within two hours.** They were hired as fully remote and get no transition period. Does anything apply to them, such as notice, travel or parking support, or a conversation about their contract? Did their offer letters say \"remote\"? They are the readers most likely to feel a promise has been broken.\n2. **Exceptions.** Is there a way to ask for an exception for caregiving, health or disability? If so, who should people talk to, and by when?\n3. **The other 31 staff.** What do they do now, for example two days in or whatever they choose? Is there enough desk and meeting space for 46 people on the same three days? People will check this.\n4. **What the office days are for.** Have you committed to anything that makes Tuesday to Thursday worth the trip, such as design reviews, pairing juniors with project leads, or interview rehearsals for bids? Without that, the reasons read as blame rather than a plan.\n5. **Logistics.** Who signs it: all three partners, or you alone? What time is Friday's meeting? Will there be one-to-one conversations afterwards, and with whom?\n6. **What staff already know or will find out.** Is there something the email leaves out that they will notice? For example: people who have recently left, how solid the 30% figure is, whether the policy will be reviewed later, or how lost bids have been talked about before.\n\nIf you don't know an answer, tell me and I'll write the email without that point rather than guess."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":628,"cache_read_input_tokens":9539,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":628},"output_to